    "ls|LS17_S15_U_177": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 177  TO BE ANSWERED ON : 02.02. 2024 \n \nBudget Outlay for Fertilizers \n \n177:  SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL: \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of Budget outlay for Fertilizers in the country during the last three \nyears, year-wise;  \n(b) the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government for balanced and \nintegrated use of inorganic and organic fertilizer s to maintain good soil \nhealth that results in increase in production in the country;  \n(c) whether the Government provides financial assistance to States/UTs for \ntraining of farmers, demonstrations on farmer’s fields and organising farmer \nmelas about the balanced use of fertilizers in the country; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \n(SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA) \n \n(a):   The details of budget outlay for Fertilizers in the country during the last three \nyears (FY 2021-22 to 2023-24) is as follows: \nYear Amount (in Crores) \n2021-22 162072.1 \n2022-23 254798.9 \n2023-24 201121 \n \n(b):   The Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) in its meeting held on \n28th June, 2023 has approved the “PM Programme for Restoration, Awareness \nGeneration, Nourishment, and Amelioration of Mother -Earth (PM -PRANAM)”. The \ninitiative aims to complement the efforts initiated by States/UTs to save the health \nof Mother Earth by promoting sustainable and balanced use of fertilizers, adopting \nalternate fertilizers, promoting organic farming. Further, regular meetings are \nundertaken with the States/UTs for promoti on of balanced use of fertilizers, use of \nalternate fertilizer like nano/bio/organic fertilizers etc. Also, promotion of PM -\nPRANAM scheme has been included as one of the activities under the Viksit Bharat \nSankalp Yatra (VBSY) which was launched on 15th November, 2023. Department of \nFertilizers is also promoting the scheme through different platforms. \n\n-2- \n \nThe Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) is also recommending \nsoil test based balanced and integrated nutrient management through conjunctive \nuse of both inorganic and organic sources (manure, bio -fertilizers, green manuring, \nin-situ crop residue recycling etc.) of plant nutrients with 4R approach i.e., right \nquantity, right time, right mode and right type of fertilizer for judicious use of \nchemical fertilizers and to reduce use of chemical fertilizers. \n \n(c) & (d):  Government is implementing the Soil Health & Fertility of Rashtriya Krishi \nVikas Yojana (RKVY) in the country including Ladakh. Under the scheme, since, \n2014-15, so far 23.74 crore soil  health cards have been issued and also providing \nfinancial assistance to States/UTs for training of farmers, demonstrations on \nfarmer's fields and organizing farmers mela about the balanced use of fertilizers in \nthe country. Based on the recommendations o n Soil Health Card (SHC), the \ntrainings & demonstrations are conducted to create the awareness among the \nfarmers for adopting the recommendations on judicious use of chemical fertilizers \nincluding secondary and micronutrients in conjunction with organic ma nures & bio -\nfertilizers. So far, 93781 farmer's trainings, 6.45 lakh demonstrations, 7425 farmer's \nmelas/campaigns on soil health card recommendations have been organized \nacross the country. \n \n \n \n******** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S15_U_178": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 178 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2nd February, 2024 \n  \nAchievements Under PMBJP  \n \n178.  SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA:  \nSHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of achievements made under Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadi Pariyojana \n(PMBJP) since its inception;  \n(b) the current progress of the PMBJP along with the schemes being evolved for its expansion \nin future;  \n(c) the type of medicines and the number of diseases for which medicines are being made \navailable for sale through the PMBJP in the country;  \n(d) the number of centres opened under PMBJP i n different districts of Gujarat and \nMaharashtra so far;  \n(e) whether the Government has taken/proposes to take any action to promote the PMBJP \nScheme in the country; and  \n(f) if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n (SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA)   \n                             \n(a) & (b): With an objective of making quality generic medicines available at affordable prices \nto all especially the poor and the deprived ones, Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushad hi \nPariyojana (PMBJP) was launched by the government in the year 2008. Under the scheme, \ndedicated outlets known as Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Kendras (PMBJKs) are \nopened all over the country to provide generic medicines to the masses.  \n  \nTill 31.12.2023, about 10,454 PMBJKs have been opened across the country. Prices of \nthe Jan Aushadhi medicines are generally 50%-90% less than that of branded medicines’ prices \nwhich are available in the open market. During the past 9 years, estimated savings of more than \nRs. 28,000 crores for the citizens have been possible. \n  \nFor further expansion of the scheme, Government has set a target for opening of 25,000 \nJAKs across the country by 31 st March 2026. Accordingly, PMBI  has invited online \napplications from all districts of the country.   \n  \n(c):  The product basket of PMBJP comprises 1965 medicines and 293 surgicals/ consumables \ncovering all major therapeutic groups such as Cardiovascular, Anti -cancers, Anit-diabetics, \nAnti-infectives, Anti -allergic, Gastro -intestinal medicines etc.   In addition to this, various \nnutraceutical products like Protein Powder, Malt -based Food Supplements, etc. and some \n\nAYUSH products like Ayuraksha Kit, Balraksha Kit and Ayush-64 tablet as Immunity boosters \nhave been added in the product basket of the Pariyojana which is being made available through \nselected Kendras.  \n  \n(d): Under Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP), till 31.12.2023, 10454 \nPradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Kendras (PMBJKs) have been opened across the country \nincluding 587 Kendras in the State of Maharashtra and 624 Kendras opened in the State of \nGujarat. Districts-wise lists of PMBJKs opened in Maharashtra and Gujarat till 31.12.2023 are \nenclosed as Annexure-I and Annexure-II respectively. \n \n(e) & (f) : Pharmaceuticals & Medical Devices Bureau of India ( PMBI), the implementing \nagency of Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP) is spreading awareness \nabout the features of PMBJP and benefits of Jan A ushadhi generic medicines through various \ntypes of advertisements such as Print Media, Radio advertisements, TV advertisements, \nCinema Advertisements and Outdoor publicity like Hoardings, Bus Queue Shelter branding, \nBus branding, Auto wrapping, etc. In addition to this, PMBI is also educating the public about \nthe benefits of Jan Aushadhi generic medicines through social media platforms like Facebook, \nTwitter, Instagram, YouTube, etc. regularly. \n \nFurthermore, PMBI is also educating citizens of the country ab out the benefits of Jan \nAushadhi generic medicines by organizing Jan Aushadhi Diwas every year on 7 th March. \nWorkshops and seminars are being organized during various event celebrations such as Azadi \nKa Amrit Mahotsav, National Unity Day week, etc. to educ ate the citizens about PMBJP \nScheme. \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure- I  \n \nStatement referred to in reply to part (d) of the LOK SABHA UNSTARRED Q. NO. 178 \nfor answer on 02.02.2024 raised by SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA, SHRI SUNIL \nDATTATRAY TATKARE regarding Achievements Under PMBJP \n  \nDistrict-wise list of PMBJKs opened in Maharashtra till 31.12.2023 \nSl. No. Name of the District Number of PMBJKs Opened \n1 Ahmednagar 13 \n2 Akola 11 \n3 Amravati 10 \n4 Aurangabad_Mh 11 \n5 Beed 30 \n6 Bhandara 2 \n7 Buldhana 30 \n8 Chandrapur 5 \n9 Dhule 6 \n10 Gadchiroli 1 \n11 Gondia 4 \n12 Hingoli 7 \n13 Jalgaon 13 \n14 Jalna 29 \n15 Kolhapur 18 \n16 Latur 44 \n17 Mumbai 57 \n18 Mumbai Suburban 9 \n19 Nagpur 13 \n20 Nanded 21 \n21 Nandurbar 4 \n22 Nashik 16 \n23 Osmanabad 14 \n24 Palghar 19 \n25 Parbhani 15 \n26 Pune 40 \n27 Raigad 9 \n28 Ratnagiri 2 \n29 Sangli 26 \n30 Satara 14 \n31 Sindhudurg 2 \n32 Solapur 23 \n33 Thane 50 \n34 Wardha 4 \n35 Washim 6 \n36 Yavatmal 9 \nTotal 587 \n  \n   \n  \n\nAnnexure- II \n  \nStatement referred to in reply to part (d) of the LOK SABHA UNSTARRED Q. NO. 178 \nfor answer on 02.02.2024 raised by SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA, SHRI SUNIL \nDATTATRAY TATKARE regarding Achievements Under PMBJP \n  \nDistrict wise list of PMBJKs opened in Gujarat till 31.12.2023 \nSl. No. Name of the District No. of PMBJKs Opened \n1 Ahmadabad 75 \n2 Amreli 14 \n3 Anand 18 \n4 Arvalli 6 \n5 Banas Kantha 14 \n6 Bharuch 14 \n7 Bhavnagar 25 \n8 Botad 10 \n9 Chhotaudepur 6 \n10 Dang 4 \n11 Devbhumi Dwarka 5 \n12 Dohad 5 \n13 Gandhinagar 11 \n14 Gir Somnath 12 \n15 Jamnagar 16 \n16 Junagadh 22 \n17 Kachchh 23 \n18 Kheda 19 \n19 Mahesana 18 \n20 Mahisagar 7 \n21 Morbi 8 \n22 Narmada 5 \n23 Navsari 8 \n24 Panch Mahals 9 \n25 Patan 14 \n26 Porbandar 7 \n27 Rajkot 68 \n28 Sabar Kantha 17 \n29 Surat 90 \n30 Surendranagar 10 \n31 Tapi 6 \n32 Vadodara 47 \n33 Valsad 11 \nTotal 624 \n  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S15_U_179": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.-179 \nANSWERED ON 02/02/2024 \n \nAYUSH Medical Colleges \n \n179.   SHRI RAMCHARAN BOHRA: \n \nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether there is shortage of AYUSH medical colleges in the country and if so, the details \nthereof State/UT-wise and discipline-wise along with the number of such functioning colleges; \n \nb) the details of the funds provided to these colleges during the last three y ears and current \nyear; \n \nc) whether the Government proposes to set up new AYUSH medical colleges in the country \nand if so, the details thereof,  State/UT-wise and location-wise including Rajasthan; \n \nd) whether the Government has received proposals from vari ous States/UTs including \nRajasthan for setting up of AYUSH Medical Colleges; and \n \ne) if so, the details thereof along with the action taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AYUSH \n \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n(a) The details of functioning AYUSH medical colleges in the country, State/UT-wise and \ndiscipline-wise are placed at Annexure I. \n \n(b)  State/UT-wise details of the funds approved under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of \nNational Ayush Mission (NAM)  to the  AYUSH colleges during the last three years and the \ncurrent year are placed at Annexure-II. \n \n(c), (d) & (e) Public health is a State subject and accordingly setting up of AYUSH medical \ncolleges comes under purview of respective State/UT Governments includi ng Rajasthan. \nHowever, under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National Ayush Mission (NAM), there is a \nprovision of providing financial assistance for the establishment of new Ayush colleges in the \nStates where availability of Ayush teaching institutions is inadequate in Government Sector. In \nthis regard, State/UT Governments are required to submit their proposal through State An nual \nAction Plans (SAAPs). As per the proposals received from State/UT Governments including \n\nRajasthan through SAAPs, State -UT-wise details of approved new Ayush Educational \nInstitutions under NAM (as on 31.01.2024) are placed at Annexure III. \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure I \n \nThe numbers of functioning AYUSH medical colleges in the country, State/UT-wise and \ndiscipline-wise \n \n \nS. No. State/UT Ayurveda Homoeopathy Sidhha Unani Sowa -Rigpa \n1. Andhra Pradesh 03 07 - 01 - \n2. Arunachal Pradesh - 01 - - - \n3. Assam 01 03 - - - \n4. Bihar 10 15 - 05 - \n5. Chandigarh 01 01 - - - \n6. Chhattisgarh 06 03 - 01 - \n7. New Delhi 03 03 - 02 - \n8. Goa 02 01 - - - \n9. Gujarat 45 47 - - - \n10. Haryana 14 01 - - - \n11. Himachal Pradesh 04 01 - - 01 \n12. Jammu and Kashmir 02 - - 04 - \n13. Jharkhand 01 06 - - - \n14. Karnataka 99 19 - 06 01 \n15. Kerala 18 06 01 01 - \n16. Madhya Pradesh 33 25 - 04 - \n17. Maharashtra 120 66 - 07 - \n18. Meghalaya 02 01 - - - \n19. Odisha 06 07 - - - \n20. Punjab 18 04 - 01 - \n21. Rajasthan 18 12 - 03 - \n22. Tamil Nadu 08 14 16 01 - \n23. Telangana 02 06 - 03 - \n24. Uttar Pradesh 100 14 - 17 01 \n25. Uttarakhand 20 02 - 01 - \n26. West Bengal 04 12 - 01 01 \n27. Pondicherry 01 - - - - \n28. Sikkim - - - - 01 \n29. Ladakh - - - - 02 \nTotal 541 277 17 58 07 \n\nDetails of Ayurveda Colleges in the Country, State/UT- wise  \n \nS.No State College  Name/Details Type \n1 \nAndhra \nPradesh \nSri V enkateshwara Ayurvedic College, SVIMS \nCampus,   T T Devasthanam,  Tirupati North -\n517507,Andhra Pradesh \nPrivate \n2 \nDr. NR S hastry Govt. Ayurvedic College, M.G. \nRoad, Vijayawada -520002, Urban Mandal, Krishna \nDistrict. Andhra Pradesh \nGovernment \n3 \nSri Adi Shiva Sadguru Alli Saheb Sivaaryula \nAyurvedic Medic al College and Research Centre, \nGuntakal-515801, Anantapur, Andhar Pradesh \nPrivate \n4 Assam Government Ayurvedic College,  Jalukbari, Distt -\nKamrup (Metro),  Guwahati- 781014 , Assam Government \n5 \nBihar \nNitishwar Ayur ved Medical C